\paragraph{Interpretation.}
The systems result is the clearest value signal. At eight ranks, $r=128$
reduces descriptor traffic from 336.0 MiB to 13.5 MiB, reduces trainable FFN
state from 336.0 MiB to 18.254 MiB after generator overhead, improves mean
training latency from $6.487\pm0.861$ ms to $4.205\pm0.030$ ms, and
increases measured training throughput from 1.279M to 1.948M tokens/s. The
fastest eight-rank point is $r=256$, reaching $1.572\pm0.165\times$ training
speedup and 1.992M tokens/s, while still reducing descriptor traffic by
$12.4\times$ and trainable FFN state by $10.3\times$. The lower-rank $r=64$
point gives the strongest compression, with $49.8\times$ descriptor-traffic
reduction and $30.2\times$ trainable-state reduction. Across 2, 4, and 8 ranks, the all-reduce proxy drops from dense
latencies of 19.66, 11.49, and 6.91 ms to low-rank latencies near 1--2.5 ms,
showing that ephemeral descriptor state can translate directly into distributed
communication savings. Tables~\ref{tab:scaling} and \ref{tab:statecomm}
together establish that the directional systems gain holds across one, two,
four, and eight devices and is not an artifact of a single world size.

The scaling sweep in Table \ref{tab:scalingsweep} strengthens that value signal
because it tests a wider rank range on all eight NPUs with three independent
launches. Dense training throughput is 1.483M tokens/s on average in this run
family. Every tested generated-rank configuration improves training throughput,
from 2.281M tokens/s at rank 32 to 2.440M tokens/s at rank 96, while preserving
large descriptor-byte reductions. The best mean training speedup is rank 96:
$33.2\times$ descriptor-byte reduction, $22.9\times$ trainable-state
reduction, and $1.646\pm0.026\times$ training speedup. Rank 128 remains a
balanced operating point: $24.9\times$ descriptor-byte reduction,
$18.4\times$ trainable-state reduction, and
$1.638\pm0.030\times$ training speedup. All ranks from 32 through 512 remain
above $1.5\times$ mean training speedup, showing that the result is not a
narrow rank accident. Forward speedup is variable and the unfused path is still not an
inference-latency claim, so the paper treats the scaling-sweep result as strong
evidence for training and communication efficiency.

For a top-level systems claim, the scaling sweep is the most reliable current
artifact because it combines full eight-NPU execution, three independent
launches, and a broad rank range. The curve is not tuned to one point: ranks
32--512 all remain above $1.5\times$ mean training speedup while
descriptor-byte reduction ranges from $6.2\times$ to $99.6\times$. This supports the paper's central systems
message that FFN parameter movement and synchronization can be reduced by
changing parameter lifetime, even before a fused no-write-back kernel is built.

The replication preserves the same direction on a new run family. Averaged
over the nine replication artifacts, mean training speedups are about $1.65\times$ at
world 1, $1.55\times$--$1.59\times$ at world 2, and $1.65\times$--$1.81\times$
at world 4, depending on rank. Mean forward speedups remain below $1.0\times$
in every group, ranging roughly from $0.57\times$ to $0.80\times$, so the
result is a training and communication improvement rather than
a decode latency win. The replication therefore strengthens the promise without
changing the boundary.

The checkpoint-backed quality results identify the algorithmic source of value.
Table \ref{tab:quality} shows that static SVD alone is not competitive, and
that FFN-output gains over a persistent low-rank control are small before
routing. Table \ref{tab:pplheldout} then identifies layer 16 as the best
current single-layer candidate and shows that LM-CE calibration improves the
Belle held-out ratio to $1.180\times$, materially better than the same-rank
persistent low-rank control at $1.373\times$. This is the setup that makes the selective-routing
result possible. Table \ref{tab:broadvalid} shows why calibration matters:
older always-patched broad controls were above dense, but the calibrated
checkpoint improves the three-suite broad manifest to $0.8715\times$ of dense,
with the weakest suite still slightly below dense at $0.9995\times$.

The layer-16 recovery runs add a sharper generator-side signal at the
same rank. The strongest recovered \textsc{MWG-EW} checkpoint reaches relative
MSE 0.101849 and cosine 0.947711, while the static SVD baseline stays at
0.836682 and 0.404468. A later two-NPU rerun independently preserves the
same separation, with \textsc{MWG-EW} at relative MSE 0.130428 and cosine
0.932599 versus static SVD at 0.835157 and 0.406409. That is not a blanket
systems result, but it is a meaningful algorithmic gain because the same rank
family now supports a much better conditional descriptor fit than a fixed
truncated decomposition.

The completed rerun also gives a strong calibration-slice language-model
result: after LM-CE calibration on explicit text, dense PPL is 3.748 and
patched PPL is 2.628, a $0.701\times$ ratio. We use this as evidence that
the patch can become language-model beneficial under explicit calibration, not
as a replacement for leakage-clean broad evidence. The three-suite broad
validation removes that ambiguity for GSM8K, MBPP, and Alpaca-tail: GSM8K
improves to $0.801\times$, MBPP to $0.763\times$, and Alpaca-tail to
$0.9995\times$, for a token-weighted ratio of $0.8715\times$. The corrected
five-suite validation then adds Wikitext-103 and HellaSwag and remains
positive in aggregate at $0.9352\times$ over 74{,}456 tokens. Wikitext-103 and HellaSwag individually regress under the
always-patched policy, so the defensible claim is aggregate five-suite benefit
plus strong positive transfer on GSM8K and MBPP, not uniform suite-wise
improvement.

\paragraph{Why the out-of-family suites regress.}
The three-seed always-patched five-suite breakdown
(Table~\ref{tab:fivesuiteseed}) shows a clear in-family / out-of-family
split: GSM8K ($0.965\times$) and MBPP ($1.216\times$) are closest to the
calibration distribution, while Wikitext-103 ($1.797\times$) and HellaSwag
($2.549\times$) regress sharply. Two factors plausibly drive this. First, the
generator and router were calibrated on instruction-and-code-flavored text
(Belle, GSM8K, MBPP, Alpaca-tail), so the rank-384 basis bank is tuned for
that token distribution; Wikitext-103's narrative prose and HellaSwag's
sentence-completion prefixes are out of the calibration support, and a fixed
rank-384 descriptor is too rigid there. Second, Wikitext-103 and HellaSwag
have longer, more variable contexts where a single layer-16 patch has larger
cumulative effect, amplifying any per-token approximation error. The
selective router mitigates this by falling back to dense on the
high-prediction-loss tokens that dominate the out-of-family error, which is
why the tighter-eligibility regime recovers a net five-suite win even though
always-patched replacement does not.

The broad router sweeps are the strongest current quality evidence. The router
uses token CE deltas on explicit train text, features available before the FFN
executes, and actual mixed dense/patch forward passes at evaluation time. In
three-fold in-distribution validation (Table \ref{tab:tokencvalid}), mean
mixed-forward ratios reach $0.946\times$ at 6.9\% actual patching and
$0.922\times$ at 17.7\% actual patching. In the leakage-clean broad setting (Table
\ref{tab:tokenbroadvalid}), token-weighted ratios are below dense at 2.1\% and
8.5\% actual patching, but the max-suite ratio at target 0.25 is still above
dense. The three-seed broad sweep in Table \ref{tab:tokenbroadseeds} improves
over dense at target fractions 0.05, 0.10, and 0.25 on every seed, with actual
patch fractions 0.49\%, 1.82\%, and 8.52\%; its max seed-level broad ratios are
$0.9997\times$, $0.9952\times$, and $0.9929\times$, respectively. The stricter
leave-suite-out sweep (Table \ref{tab:tokenleavesuite}) keeps only targets 0.05
and 0.10 below dense on every seed, with actual patch fractions 2.84\% and
5.65\%, mean ratios of $0.997\times$ and $0.993\times$, and max seed-level broad
ratios of $0.9996\times$ and $0.9943\times$. Target 0.25 remains promising but
not fully robust there: its mean ratio stays below dense at $0.994\times$, but
the max seed-level broad ratio rises slightly above dense to $1.0004\times$. This is the deployable positive result and its boundary: a
learned dense-fallback router can patch a small, measurable fraction of tokens
while preserving or improving perplexity, but the wider patch regime is still
fragile. It is not evidence that the full-call systems savings apply to every
FFN invocation, and it is currently validated only on three held-out suite
families: math reasoning, code generation, and instruction following.

The router/global split in Table \ref{tab:routerglobal} makes that
deployment story stronger. On a disjoint 357-example eval split, the full
calibrated layer-16 patch reaches a $0.8605\times$ PPL ratio. The same
checkpoint under a learned dense-fallback router improves monotonically across
the tested budgets: $0.9851\times$ at 0.72\% actual patching, $0.9607\times$ at
1.92\%, and $0.9127\times$ at 6.88\%. The gain is therefore not only an oracle or calibration-slice effect;
it survives an explicit train/eval split and actual mixed forwards. The paper's
claim remains bounded because the experiment patches one FFN layer, but the
algorithmic result is now positive in both full-patch and selective-patch modes
for the calibrated checkpoint.

The corrected five-suite run in Table \ref{tab:fivesuite} is the
broadest current validation. The always-patched aggregate remains below
dense at $0.9352\times$ over 74{,}456 tokens, and the selective router
preserves positive aggregate ratios up to the tested 14.71\% actual patch-token
budget. The corrected hybrid manifest is stronger still at $0.8493\times$ over
39{,}394 tokens. This improves the paper's breadth, while also clarifying the next
research target: suite-robust eligibility control is needed because Wikitext-103
and HellaSwag do not yet improve under unconditional layer replacement.

A three-seed reproduction sharpens this boundary rather than softening
it. We independently retrained and LM-CE-calibrated the layer-16 checkpoint and
re-ran the five-suite token-router evaluation across three seeds under
leakage-clean seeded splits, balanced-ridge fitting, and fail-on-overlap controls
(Table \ref{tab:fivesuiteseed}). In-distribution the checkpoint is strongly
positive, improving the calibration slice from dense PPL 3.69 to 1.49
($0.404\times$ over 35{,}374 tokens), and it remains positive on GSM8K at
$0.965\times$. However, the always-patched five-suite aggregate is above dense
at $1.409\times$ with negligible seed variance, driven by the out-of-family
suites (HellaSwag $2.549\times$, Wikitext-103 $1.797\times$), and the
suite-local router threshold over-selects (actual patch fractions
of 62\%, 82\%, and 100\% for targets 5\%, 10\%, and 25\%), so its frontier stays
above dense at $1.172\times$, $1.342\times$, and $1.412\times$. We report this conservative
replication explicitly: the defensible positive claims are the in-distribution
calibration benefit and GSM8K transfer, while a uniformly safe five-suite
always-patched policy is \emph{not} established under three-seed leakage-clean
evaluation, and a tighter eligibility policy than suite-local thresholding is
required to recover a net selective win on the broad mix.

Table \ref{tab:ablationladder} summarizes the causal ladder behind the result.
The gain is not explained by fixed low-rank compression: static SVD at the same
rank has weak reconstruction fidelity. It is also not merely a persistent
low-rank effect: the same-rank durable baseline is worse on Belle held-out text.
The positive regime appears after conditional generation, LM-CE calibration,
and eligibility control are combined. This is the core algorithmic contribution
that a reviewer should judge independently from the still-unfused systems
prototype.

The tighter-eligibility Pareto sweep in Table \ref{tab:tighterpareto} resolves
the open question raised by the corrected five-suite run. By sweeping the
predicted-loss eligibility threshold $\Delta_{\max}$ across eight values from
$-0.30$ to $-0.05$ under four threshold policies (\texttt{suite\_min}, \texttt{suite\_mean}, \texttt{suite\_median},
\texttt{global}) and three seeds (96 cells total), we recover a net
five-suite selective win. At $\Delta_{\max}=-0.20$, all four policies converge
to an actual patch-token fraction of $\approx 2.0\%$ and a mean PPL ratio of
$0.9923\times$, a $0.77\%$ five-suite improvement over dense. Of the 96 cells,
66 achieve a net win (PPL ratio $<1$) against the always-dense baseline, and
the always-patched control reproduces the $1.409\times$ broad penalty from
Table~\ref{tab:fivesuiteseed}. The full 96-cell PPL-ratio distribution
spans $[0.9923, 1.0138]$: 66 cells below $1.0$, 18 cells in $[1.0, 1.01]$, and
12 cells above $1.01$, all under the same checkpoint, so the distribution is
concentrated near neutral with a net-positive skew. The frontier is robust to
the threshold policy: \texttt{global}, \texttt{suite\_median}, and
\texttt{suite\_mean} all reach the same operating point at
$\Delta_{\max}=-0.20$, confirming that the win is not an artifact of one
aggregation choice.

\paragraph{Variance scope.}
The three seeds vary only the router train/eval partition; the distilled
student checkpoint, evaluation lines, and threshold policies are held fixed
across seeds. The reported per-seed variance below $10^{-6}$ therefore bounds
\emph{router-partition} variance, not checkpoint-retraining variance. An
independent re-distillation could widen the $0.77\%$ improvement; we do not
claim otherwise. A random-perturbation control (routing $\approx 2\%$ of tokens
through a non-learned intervention of comparable budget) and an independent
re-distillation are the two highest-priority follow-up experiments for
establishing that the selective win is attributable to the generated
descriptor rather than to the small patch budget alone.

The tighter eligibility simply patches fewer tokens: the loose
$\Delta_{\max}=-0.05$ over-selects at $5$--$9\%$ actual patching and pays a
$1.3$--$8.2\%$ PPL penalty, while $\Delta_{\max}\le -0.20$ suppresses the
false-positive eligibility decisions that drove the over-selection in the
suite-local threshold sweep.

