Validation setting & Target & Actual patch frac. & Full-call red. & Effective routed bytes \\
\midrule
Broad seed sweep & 0.05 & 0.49\% & 24.9x & 12.2\% of dense \\
Broad seed sweep & 0.10 & 1.82\% & 24.9x & 45.3\% of dense \\
Leave-suite-out & 0.05 & 2.84\% & 24.9x & 70.7\% of dense \\
Leave-suite-out & 0.10 & 5.65\% & 24.9x & 140.6\% of dense \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The implication of Table~\ref{tab:effectivesavings} is that the full-call
descriptor-byte reduction cannot be naively multiplied by the patch fraction
to obtain an end-to-end routed saving. At the small actual patch fractions
where the selective router is quality-preserving (0.49\% and 1.82\% in the
broad seed sweep), the effective routed descriptor bytes are only 12.2\% and
45.3\% of dense---a real reduction, but far smaller than the $24.9\times$
per-call figure. Under the stricter leave-suite-out sweep, the target 0.10 row is
\emph{above} dense (140.6\%), illustrating why the paper scopes its systems
claim to the full-call and training settings and treats end-to-end routed
savings as an open target.

Table \ref{tab:runtimeprobe} records the remaining systems instrumentation
scope. On the eight-NPU host, the PyTorch profiler exposed CPU activities
only and observed no fused-like MWG operator. The paper therefore reports
descriptor traffic as a model-side estimate paired with measured latency
and all-reduce behavior, not as measured off-chip traffic. Off-chip counter
confirmation is a required future fused-kernel experiment.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\textsc{MWG-EW} is best understood as a bridge between neural architecture and
runtime design. The neural component makes a low-rank conditional descriptor
useful on some inputs; the systems component ensures that this descriptor does
not quietly become another persistent tensor. Either side alone is insufficient.
Static low-rank compression gives attractive byte counts but weak quality, while
an unfused runtime can expose the right trend without proving the final
no-write-back traffic claim.

The most important conceptual shift is from compression to \textit{lifetime
allocation}. A dense FFN weight is a long-lived object. A LoRA factor is smaller
but still long-lived. An ephemeral descriptor is short-lived and context-bound.
This gives model designers a new budget: not merely how many weights exist, but
which weight-like objects deserve durability. For very large models, that budget
is directly tied to memory bandwidth, optimizer state, checkpoint traffic, and
collective communication.

The quality results suggest a concrete algorithmic agenda. The strongest
current algorithm is not ``replace every FFN,'' but ``learn the eligibility
frontier.'' Better benefit predictors, suite-local thresholds, layer-specific
calibration, rank-adaptive descriptors, and multi-layer budget allocation can
increase the fraction of FFN calls that safely use generated operands. In this
view, the router is not an auxiliary patch; it is the mechanism that turns a
conditional systems primitive into a deployable model component. The positive
result is already useful because it converts a failing always-patched
replacement into a verified selective operating regime.

\paragraph{Relation to KV-cache compression and MLA.}
As detailed in Section~\ref{sec:related}, \textsc{MWG-EW} is orthogonal to
attention-side KV-cache compression methods: it targets the lifecycle of FFN
weight operands rather than the representation of past-token activations, and
the two savings compose rather than overlap.

\paragraph{Current scope and submission readiness.}
The present evidence supports a positive systems-and-routing claim suitable for
a focused architecture or systems submission. The paper verifies four linked
claims: generated descriptors can substantially reduce trainable FFN state and
communication, conditional generation beats static SVD at the same rank, LM-CE
calibration can make the best single-layer patch beneficial on broad
perplexity, and a deployable dense-fallback router preserves additional gains
at low actual patch fractions. Descriptor lifecycle claims still require
low-level hardware performance counters for off-chip read and write
transactions. The replication produced a verified nine-run reliability artifact
set, the scaling sweep produced a three-repeat all-eight-NPU sweep over ranks 32--512, the
corrected validation produced a positive five-suite aggregate PPL result
for the calibrated layer-16 checkpoint, and a three-seed leakage-clean
reproduction confirmed strong in-distribution and GSM8K benefit while showing
that the always-patched five-suite aggregate is not yet uniformly safe under
seeded splits.
The main claim boundary is therefore precise: the paper establishes
\textsc{MWG-EW} as a parameter-lifetime and selective execution contribution,
not yet as a fused decode-latency win or a drop-in full-Transformer
replacement. The remaining highest-value follow-up experiments are
fused-kernel hardware-counter validation and multi-layer budget allocation;
the suite-robust eligibility gap has already been closed by the
tighter-eligibility Pareto sweep reported in Section~\ref{sec:results}.

\paragraph{Future work.}
Three directions follow directly from the evidence map
(Table~\ref{tab:evidencemap}). First, a fused no-write-back kernel with
hardware performance counters is needed to
turn the analytically estimated descriptor-traffic reduction into a measured
off-chip traffic claim; this is the critical path for decode-latency
validation. Second, per-layer calibration and multi-layer budget allocation
across the FFN stack would test whether the layer-16 selective win generalizes
beyond a single layer; the negative layer-17/18 results
(Table~\ref{tab:multilayerpareto}) indicate that a naive transfer is
insufficient and that a per-layer eligibility policy is required. Third,
rank-adaptive descriptors that adjust the generated rank per token group could
improve the quality-cost frontier beyond the fixed-rank sweep studied here.
Longer-horizon targets include extension to attention projection weights,
dialogue and long-context evaluation suites, and joint optimization of the
generator and router under a unified bandwidth budget.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
We state the boundary of the present evidence explicitly. First, the systems
measurements use an \emph{unfused} associative factor-matmul path; the
no-write-back descriptor lifecycle is a specified runtime contract, not yet a
measured off-chip behavior, because the available profiler exposed CPU
activities only and observed no fused-like operator. Concretely, the
validation target for the no-write-back claim is a fused kernel whose HBM
read/write byte counters for the descriptor region are bounded by the
$2r(d+m)$ per-projection descriptor size and show no write-back of generated
factor tiles to off-chip memory; we have not yet run this experiment. Second,
the validated systems claim is scoped to training throughput, trainable state,
and distributed communication; the prototype is not faster on the unfused
forward path, so no decode-latency claim is made. Third, the language-model quality
evidence is anchored on a single calibrated FFN layer (layer 16) of one
checkpoint family (Qwen2.5-1.5B); multi-layer and multi-model generalization is
future work. Fourth, always-patched replacement is broadly safe only for the
calibrated layer-16 checkpoint and only in aggregate over the five-suite
manifest, with Wikitext-103 and HellaSwag individually regressing; the robust
positive result is the \emph{selective}, budgeted router regime at small actual
patch fractions, and the tighter-eligibility Pareto sweep
(Table~\ref{tab:tighterpareto}) establishes that such a net five-suite win is
recoverable at $\Delta_{\max}=-0.20$ under a policy-invariant threshold.
Fifth, the broad held-out manifest currently covers math
reasoning, code generation, and instruction following plus two added general/
commonsense suites, but does not yet cover dialogue or long-context families,
so we do not make a broad-family deployment claim. Sixth, all reported quality
metrics are token-level perplexity (or cosine/MSE on layer-output recovery);
no downstream task accuracy (e.g., MMLU, HumanEval pass@1, GSM8K final-answer
accuracy) is reported, so the perplexity improvements shown here have not yet
been linked to end-task wins. This is the single most important follow-up
for the quality claim: a $0.77\%$ PPL improvement at $2\%$ patching could
correspond to no change---or even a regression---in downstream accuracy, and
PPL is a particularly weak proxy for generation-quality tasks where a small
number of high-impact tokens dominate the metric. We therefore flag GSM8K
final-answer exact-match and HumanEval pass@1 on the layer-16 selective regime
as the top-priority next experiment, ahead of the broader suite expansion.
We report all of these as boundary evidence rather than omitting the negative
controls.

\paragraph{Reproducibility.}
Every reported number is tied to a named artifact. The systems sweeps are kept
under explicit run identifiers (the all-eight-NPU scaling sweep and the
nine-run reliability set), each recording
platform metadata, device ids, and per-launch JSON. Quality and routing runs
use explicit train/eval text paths, an explicit-text guard that rejects
default-corpus fallback, manifest-driven corpora with minimum-size and
overlap checks, and seeded train/eval partitions. We
report descriptor traffic as a model-side analytic estimate derived from the
measured FFN geometry and clearly separate it from measured latency and
all-reduce numbers in the evidence map (Table~\ref{tab:evidencemap}). All
benchmark, distillation, calibration, router, and broad-validation scripts,
together with the held-out manifest definitions and pulled result summaries,
are archived with named run identifiers. We commit to a public artifact
release of the benchmark, distillation, calibration, router, and
broad-validation scripts, together with the held-out manifest definitions and
seeded partition generators, upon acceptance; the named run identifiers above
permit exact replay of every reported number in the meantime, and the
underlying Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct checkpoint is already public.

\section{Conclusion}

This paper argues that Transformer FFN weights can be treated not only as
stored parameters, but as runtime-generated operands with explicit lifetimes.
\textsc{MWG-EW} realizes this idea with conditional rank-$r$ descriptors, an
associative execution path, and a proposed no-write-back lifecycle. Full
generated calls reduce \emph{estimated} descriptor traffic, trainable FFN
state, and distributed gradient communication in an eight-NPU 8B-scale FFN
systems benchmark. We emphasize two systems caveats that bound this claim: the
unfused prototype's forward path is slower than dense (no decode-latency win),
and the descriptor-traffic figure is an analytic estimate, not an HBM-counter
measurement; at the quality-preserving patch fractions, the effective routed
saving is $2\times$--$8\times$, not the full-call $24.9\times$.

The checkpoint-backed Qwen2.5-1.5B experiments identify a concrete
quality-preserving regime: layer 16 is the best current single-layer candidate,
LM-CE calibration improves it relative to persistent low-rank control, and a
benefit-supervised dense-fallback router preserves or improves broad perplexity
at small actual patch-token fractions across leakage-clean, leave-suite-out, and
corrected five-suite validation, recovering a net $0.77\%$ five-suite
improvement at ${\sim}2\%$ patching in the tighter-eligibility sweep. The
three-seed leakage-clean replication bounds this regime honestly: the same
checkpoint is strongly positive in-distribution and on GSM8K, but the
always-patched five-suite aggregate is above dense, so a uniformly safe
always-patched policy is not yet established. The selective win is a
calibrated single-layer existence proof (layers~17--18 do not transfer), and
its $0.77\%$ magnitude has not yet been validated against downstream-task
accuracy or against a random-perturbation control. The defensible
contribution is therefore the \emph{selective}, budgeted regime at small
actual patch fractions, not blanket layer replacement.
The broader contribution is the
parameter-lifetime view itself. The paper therefore establishes a reproducible
path for co-designing memory movement, synchronization, and model quality rather
than optimizing them in isolation.

The reliability replication and three-repeat all-NPU sweep strengthen
that path operationally: the same rank family scales repeatably across one,
two, four, and eight-device settings, and the widest rank sweep shows a
stable $1.5\times$--$1.65\times$ training-side improvement across a broad rank
range. It still does not license stronger decode claims. The highest-value
follow-up experiments are a fused no-write-back kernel with hardware-counter
confirmation of off-chip descriptor traffic, and multi-layer budget allocation
across the FFN stack. Each remaining target addresses a specific boundary
stated in the evidence map, and each is required before the parameter-lifetime
view can be turned into a deployable full-Transformer execution primitive.

